.TH ZEYPLE 1 2026-10-28 "Zeyple 3.0.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
zeyple \- Zeyple Encrypts Your Precious Log Emails
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zeyple.py
.I recipient
.RI [ recipient ...]
.B < message
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Zeyple
is a Postfix after-queue content filter that automatically encrypts
outgoing emails with GnuPG.
It catches emails from the Postfix queue, encrypts them with the public key
of each recipient it has a key for, and puts them back into the queue.
.PP
.nf
\&     unencrypted email   ||   encrypted email
sender \-\-> Postfix \-\-> Zeyple \-\-> Postfix \-\-> recipient(s)
.fi
.PP
It is meant for the emails a server sends on its own: reports and alerts
from Logwatch, Monit, Fail2ban, smartd, cron and the like, which say a lot
about the server and should not cross the network in the clear.
.PP
Postfix runs
.B zeyple.py
through the
.BR pipe (8)
delivery agent, as a dedicated
.I zeyple
user.
It reads one email from standard input and takes the envelope recipients as
arguments.
For each recipient, it looks up a key in its keyring, encrypts the email with
it and sends the result to the SMTP relay set in the configuration, which is
normally a second Postfix
.BR smtpd (8)
service that puts it back into the queue without running Zeyple again.
.PP
Encrypted emails use PGP/MIME (RFC 3156): the body and all attachments are
encrypted together into a single
.I multipart/encrypted
message.
Headers, including
.BR Subject ,
are kept as they are and stay readable.
.PP
How each recipient is handled:
.IP \(bu 2
The key is looked up by email address.
The address must match the email of one of the key's user IDs, ignoring case.
.IP \(bu 2
If no key matches and the address has a sub-address tag, such as
.IR you+tag@example.com ,
the key of
.I you@example.com
is used.
.IP \(bu 2
Keys don't need to be signed or trusted in the keyring, any key that matches
is used.
An expired key is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
Emails that are already encrypted, either PGP/MIME or a body that is nothing
but an inline PGP message, are sent as they are.
.IP \(bu 2
Without a key, the email is sent unencrypted, unless
.B force_encrypt
is set, in which case it is not sent to that recipient at all.
.PP
Each recipient gets their own SMTP transaction, with the
.B From
header as the envelope sender.
.SH OPTIONS
.B zeyple.py
has no options: every argument is a recipient address.
.TP
.I recipient
An envelope recipient of the email, as Postfix passes it with
.BR ${recipient} .
Several recipients can be given, but Zeyple is meant to run once per
recipient, see
.B zeyple_destination_recipient_limit
in
.BR "POSTFIX INTEGRATION" .
When no recipient is given, nothing is sent and a warning is logged.
.PP
The email itself is read from standard input.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Zeyple reads
.I /etc/zeyple.conf
then
.I zeyple.conf
in the current directory, and a value from the second file overrides the
first.
At least one of them must exist.
The format is INI, as read by Python's
.BR configparser .
A sample is installed as
.IR zeyple.conf.example .
.PP
Boolean options accept
.BR 1 ,
.BR yes ,
.B true
and
.B on
to enable them, and
.BR 0 ,
.BR no ,
.B false
and
.B off
to disable them.
.SS [zeyple]
.TP
.BI log_file " = path"
File to log to.
Required.
Zeyple logs at debug level: message IDs, recipients, the key used for each
recipient and the reason of every failure.
The file must be writable by the
.I zeyple
user.
.TP
.BI force_encrypt " = boolean"
Never send an email unencrypted.
When there is no key for a recipient, the email is not sent to them.
When Zeyple fails for any other reason than the relay being temporarily
unavailable, the email is dropped rather than retried, so that Postfix never
bounces it back unencrypted.
This doesn't cover failures while Zeyple starts, see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
Defaults to
.BR no ,
but the sample configuration sets it to
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BI add_header " = boolean"
Add an
.B X\-Zeyple
header to every email Zeyple sends, such as
.IR "X\-Zeyple: processed by Zeyple v3.0.0" .
Defaults to
.BR no .
.SS [gpg]
.TP
.BI home " = path"
GnuPG home directory holding the public keys of the recipients.
Required.
It must belong to the
.I zeyple
user, with mode 700.
.TP
.BI executable " = path"
Path to the
.BR gpg (1)
binary.
Defaults to the one GPGME finds on its own.
.SS [relay]
.TP
.BI host " = hostname"
SMTP server to send the processed emails to.
Required.
.TP
.BI port " = number"
Port of that SMTP server.
Required.
.PP
The connection to the relay is plain SMTP, without TLS or authentication, so
it should stay on the local host.
.SH POSTFIX INTEGRATION
Zeyple runs as a Postfix content filter.
Add the filter and the service that takes emails back from it to
.IR /etc/postfix/master.cf :
.PP
.RS
.nf
zeyple    unix  \-       n       n       \-       \-       pipe
\&  user=zeyple argv=/usr/local/bin/zeyple.py ${recipient}
.sp
localhost:10026 inet  n       \-       n       \-       10      smtpd
\&  \-o content_filter=
\&  \-o receive_override_options=no_unknown_recipient_checks,no_header_body_checks,no_milters
\&  \-o smtpd_helo_restrictions=
\&  \-o smtpd_client_restrictions=
\&  \-o smtpd_sender_restrictions=
\&  \-o smtpd_recipient_restrictions=permit_mynetworks,reject
\&  \-o mynetworks=127.0.0.0/8,[::1]/128
\&  \-o smtpd_authorized_xforward_hosts=127.0.0.0/8,[::1]/128
.fi
.RE
.PP
Then enable it in
.I /etc/postfix/main.cf
and run
.BR "postfix reload" :
.PP
.RS
.nf
content_filter = zeyple
zeyple_destination_recipient_limit = 1
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B content_filter = zeyple
Sends every email that enters the queue through Zeyple.
Comment it out and run
.B postfix reload
to disable Zeyple.
.TP
.B zeyple_destination_recipient_limit = 1
Makes Postfix run Zeyple once per recipient, so an error for one recipient
doesn't affect the others.
.TP
.B localhost:10026
The relay from the
.B [relay]
section.
Its empty
.B content_filter
keeps emails from going through Zeyple a second time.
.SH KEY MANAGEMENT
Keys live in the
.B [gpg] home
directory and are managed with
.BR gpg (1)
as the
.I zeyple
user, so that it can still read them.
.PP
List the keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo \-u zeyple gpg \-\-homedir /var/lib/zeyple/keys \-\-list\-keys
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search a keyserver and import the keys you pick at the prompt:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo \-u zeyple gpg \-\-homedir /var/lib/zeyple/keys \e
\&    \-\-keyserver hkps://keys.openpgp.org \-\-search\-keys you@example.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Import a key from a file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo \-u zeyple gpg \-\-homedir /var/lib/zeyple/keys \-\-import < key.asc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Refresh the imported keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo \-u zeyple gpg \-\-homedir /var/lib/zeyple/keys \e
\&    \-\-keyserver hkps://keys.openpgp.org \-\-refresh\-keys
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Postfix's
.BR pipe (8)
delivery agent acts on the exit status.
.TP
.B 0
The email was handled: sent to every recipient, or dropped by
.BR force_encrypt .
.TP
.BR 75 " (EX_TEMPFAIL)"
Zeyple failed: the configuration can't be read, the relay can't be reached,
a key is expired, and so on.
Postfix keeps the email in the queue and tries again later.
With
.BR force_encrypt ,
Zeyple only returns 75 when the relay is temporarily unavailable, and drops
the email otherwise.
.IP
Failures while Zeyple starts always return 75, even with
.BR force_encrypt ,
since Zeyple doesn't know its settings yet: the configuration can't be read,
has no log_file or an invalid
.B force_encrypt
value, or the log file can't be opened.
.PP
Zeyple never returns an error that makes Postfix bounce the email, as the
bounce would contain it unencrypted.
Still, an email that keeps getting 75 stays in the queue until Postfix's
.B maximal_queue_lifetime
runs out, and Postfix bounces it then.
That can happen without
.BR force_encrypt ,
or with it when Zeyple keeps failing to start or the relay stays down.
.PP
The reason of a failure is in the log file, or, when Zeyple fails before it
can open the log file, in the Postfix mail log.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/zeyple.py
The filter itself.
.TP
.I /etc/zeyple.conf
Configuration file.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/zeyple.conf.example
Sample configuration installed by the .deb package, to copy to
.IR /etc/zeyple.conf .
.TP
.I /var/lib/zeyple/keys
Default GnuPG home directory with the public keys of the recipients.
.TP
.I /var/log/zeyple.log
Default log file.
.TP
.IR /etc/postfix/master.cf ", " /etc/postfix/main.cf
Postfix configuration, see
.BR "POSTFIX INTEGRATION" .
.SH EXAMPLES
A minimal
.IR /etc/zeyple.conf :
.PP
.RS
.nf
[zeyple]
log_file = /var/log/zeyple.log
force_encrypt = 1
.sp
[gpg]
home = /var/lib/zeyple/keys
.sp
[relay]
host = localhost
port = 10026
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send a test email and check that it arrives encrypted:
.PP
.RS
.B date | mail \-s test root
.RE
.PP
Follow what Zeyple does:
.PP
.RS
.B tail \-f /var/log/zeyple.log
.RE
.PP
Encrypt emails sent to
.I root
with the key of
.IR me@example.com .
Zeyple sees the recipient before
.I /etc/aliases
is applied, so rewrite the recipient with
.B recipient_canonical_maps
instead:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo "recipient_canonical_maps = hash:/etc/postfix/recipient_canonical" >> /etc/postfix/main.cf
echo "root me@example.com" >> /etc/postfix/recipient_canonical
postmap /etc/postfix/recipient_canonical
postfix reload
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sign encrypted emails with DKIM: emails must be signed after Zeyple encrypts
them, otherwise the signature no longer matches.
Remove
.B no_milters
from the
.B localhost:10026
service in
.I /etc/postfix/master.cf
so that the signing milter, such as OpenDKIM, runs when Zeyple hands emails
back to Postfix:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&  \-o receive_override_options=no_unknown_recipient_checks,no_header_body_checks
.fi
.RE
.PP
That service is an SMTP server, so it only runs the milters in
.BR smtpd_milters .
If the signing milter is only in
.BR non_smtpd_milters ,
also add
.B \-o smtpd_milters=$non_smtpd_milters
to the service.
Then run
.BR "postfix reload" .
.SH NOTES
Zeyple is only tested with Postfix, but should work with any MTA that can
pipe emails to a filter and take them back over SMTP.
.PP
Zeyple only encrypts, it never signs or decrypts.
